// Four documents added in Session 9, covering features that did not exist when
// the original 15 were written. Kept as a separate node so this batch can be
// seeded on its own (disconnect 'Policy Documents' for a delta run -- disabling
// it is not enough, a disabled n8n node passes its input straight through); a
// full reindex runs both nodes and gets all 19.
const docs = [
  {
    doc_id: "doc_minor_guardian_accounts",
    text: "Minor and Guardian Account Policy: Digital Bank opens accounts for applicants under 18, but only with the explicit consent of a parent or guardian. Every account application must include a date of birth; we accept 2010-05-14, 14/05/2010, 14 May 2010 or May 14, 2010, and where the numeric form is ambiguous we read it day first. An applicant under 18 must also give a parent or guardian email address. We then email that guardian a consent request with a reference code beginning MIN-, and the account is only opened once the guardian replies APPROVE from that same address. Only the named guardian can answer; a reply from any other address is refused. On a guardian-supervised account the minor is the primary holder and the guardian is added as a joint holder with guardian authority. The minor can check the balance and receive money into the account at any time, but cannot transfer money out and cannot take out a loan; only the guardian can move money out. The restriction is enforced in the banking database itself, not only in the email workflow. The account converts automatically to full adult access on the holder\u2019s 18th birthday, at which point the guardian\u2019s control ends; no request or paperwork is needed for that conversion. If the guardian does not reply within 7 days the request expires and the applicant can simply ask again. If the guardian replies REJECT, no account is opened and the applicant is told."
  },
  {
    doc_id: "doc_joint_account_mandate",
    text: "Joint Account Mandate Policy: A joint account at Digital Bank operates under one of two mandates, chosen by the customers when the account is opened. Either-or (the default) means any single holder can transfer money out on their own. All-signatures, also called both-signatures or dual control, means every holder must approve each individual transfer before it is carried out. To choose the stricter mandate, say so when inviting the other person, for example \"both of us must approve every transfer\"; to choose the default, say \"either of us can act alone\". The invitation and confirmation emails both state which mandate was applied. On an all-signatures account, a requested transfer moves no money immediately: it is held, the person who asked for it is told how many approvals are outstanding, and every other holder is emailed a reference code beginning JNT- which they answer by replying APPROVE or REJECT. Only a holder of that account can answer. When the last approval arrives we run a fresh fraud check at that moment and then carry out the transfer, because the original fraud assessment expires after ten minutes. A single REJECT closes the request immediately and no money moves. An unanswered request expires after 7 days. Closing a joint account is separate from the transfer mandate and always requires the agreement of all holders, except on accounts configured for majority closure with three or more holders, where a simple majority is enough. A transfer that is fully approved but then fails, for example for insufficient funds, does not silently disappear: it stays open with the reason recorded and everyone involved is told what happened."
  },
  {
    doc_id: "doc_loan_approval_process",
    text: "Loan Approval Policy: Digital Bank offers personal loans by email at a flat 10 percent interest rate, for a term between 1 and 60 months. A loan of Rs 200,000 or less is approved automatically and the funds are credited to your account immediately. A loan above Rs 200,000 and up to the Rs 2,000,000 email ceiling is referred to our credit review team; you will receive an email confirming the principal, the term, the total repayable and the monthly repayment, and a separate email with the decision once it is made, usually within one business day. Loans above Rs 2,000,000 cannot be arranged by email and require a branch visit. Repayment is automatic: a monthly standing order is created when the loan is disbursed, and the loan is marked paid off once the outstanding balance reaches zero. You may hold one loan at a time; a further application is refused until the existing loan is repaid or the review decision on it is made. A minor account holder cannot take out a loan; a guardian must apply in their own name. If a loan is declined you are told the reason and are welcome to apply again later."
  },
  {
    doc_id: "doc_human_review_policy",
    text: "Human Review Policy: Most requests at Digital Bank are handled end to end automatically, and we deliberately do not send everything to a person. Handled without any human step: balance enquiries, transfers from accounts you hold that pass our fraud checks, deposits within the published limits, loans of Rs 200,000 or less, opening a single or joint account, scheduled standing order payments, and any policy question we can answer confidently from these published documents. A person is involved only where the decision genuinely needs judgement: a loan above Rs 200,000, a policy question our assistant cannot answer confidently from published policy, and releasing an account freeze placed by our fraud checks. In those cases nothing is sent to you until a member of our operations team has reviewed it, and you are told that a review is under way rather than being given a guess. Some decisions are not ours to make at all and are referred to you instead: a co-holder approving a transfer on an all-signatures joint account, and a parent or guardian approving an account for someone under 18. A fraud freeze cannot be lifted by the customer, by email or otherwise; that always requires a human operator, which is deliberate. Every decision, automatic or human, is written to an immutable audit record."
  },
];

return docs.map(d => ({ json: { doc_id: d.doc_id, text: d.text } }));

// One Drive file becomes one policy document. The doc_id is derived from the
// filename so it is stable across re-syncs and readable in a citation: a file
// named "01-accounts-and-eligibility.md" becomes doc_accounts_and_eligibility.
const item = $input.item.json;

const fileName = String(
  item.name || $('List Policy Documents in Drive').item.json.name || 'untitled'
);

const slug = fileName
  .replace(/\.[^.]+$/, '')          // drop the extension
  .replace(/^\d+[-_\s]*/, '')       // drop a leading ordering prefix like "01-"
  .toLowerCase()
  .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '_')
  .replace(/^_+|_+$/g, '');

// The folder also holds the README explaining how this sync works. That is
// setup documentation, not bank policy -- embedding it would let a customer's
// question retrieve an answer about Pinecone namespaces and folder IDs.
const NON_POLICY = ['readme', 'notes', 'changelog', 'todo'];
if (NON_POLICY.indexOf(slug) !== -1) {
  return { json: { doc_id: 'doc_' + slug, text: '', skipped: true, source_file: fileName } };
}

const text = String(item.text || '').trim();

// A file we could not read is skipped rather than embedded as an empty vector,
// which would pollute retrieval with a document that matches everything weakly.
if (!text) {
  return { json: { doc_id: 'doc_' + slug, text: '', skipped: true, source_file: fileName } };
}

return { json: {
  doc_id: 'doc_' + (slug || 'untitled'),
  text,
  source_file: fileName,
  skipped: false
} };

// Known fraud typologies, written the way an investigator would describe them so
// a semantic search on a real transaction shape surfaces the right one.
//
// These are ADVISORY. They are retrieved after the deterministic rules engine
// has already frozen an account, and shown to the human deciding whether to
// release it. They never feed the risk score -- the score stays deterministic
// and reproducible, which is the entire reason it is not an LLM.
//
// Each entry deliberately includes the LEGITIMATE explanations too. A pattern
// list that only describes guilt trains an operator to confirm the freeze every
// time, which is how false positives become permanent.
const patterns = [
  { doc_id: 'fraud_velocity_burst', text: 'Velocity burst or structuring. Many transfers out of one account in a short window, each small enough to look unremarkable alone. Signs: more than five transfers in an hour, similar amounts, several different destinations, often outside that customer usual hours. Legitimate explanations: settling a group bill, paying several suppliers on invoice day, a business account running payroll, or someone moving money between their own accounts. Ask whether the destinations are related to each other and whether the customer has done this before.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_account_drain', text: 'Account drain. A single transfer moving the entire or near-entire balance to one destination, frequently one never paid before, often shortly after a login or contact-detail change. This is the signature of a compromised mailbox: whoever controls the email controls the account. Legitimate explanations: paying a deposit on a house or car, closing an account and moving funds, or an emergency medical payment. Ask whether the customer can confirm the destination through a channel other than the email that requested it.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_new_beneficiary_large', text: 'Large first payment to a brand new beneficiary. The destination account has never received money from this customer and the amount is far above their normal transfer size. Strongly associated with invoice redirection fraud, where a genuine supplier email thread is hijacked and bank details swapped, and with romance and investment scams. Legitimate explanations: a genuine one-off large purchase, a new landlord, a first payment to a new supplier. Ask how the customer obtained the account details and whether they verified them by voice.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_layering_deposit_out', text: 'Layering. Money arrives and is moved straight out again with little or no time in the account, leaving a near-zero balance. The account is being used as a waypoint rather than as a place to hold money, which is the defining shape of money laundering and of a mule account. Legitimate explanations: a customer who receives their salary and immediately pays rent and bills, or a business sweeping receipts into a main account. Ask whether the inbound and outbound counterparties have any obvious relationship.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_loan_immediate_exit', text: 'Loan taken and immediately moved out. A loan is disbursed and the funds leave the account within hours, usually to a single new destination. Associated with loan-stacking fraud and with coerced borrowing, where a victim is talked into taking a loan and passing it on. Legitimate explanations: the loan was taken for a specific purchase the customer was ready to make. Ask what the loan was for and whether the destination matches that stated purpose.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_new_account_rapid_activity', text: 'Rapid activity on a very new account. Significant money movement within days of the account opening, before any normal usage pattern exists. Associated with mule accounts opened specifically to receive and forward stolen funds. Legitimate explanations: a customer who switched banks and is genuinely starting to use the new account. Ask whether the activity looks like someone living their life or like a single pass-through.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_round_number_escalation', text: 'Round-number escalation. A sequence of round-figure transfers that grow in size, often starting with a small test payment. The test confirms the account works and is not being watched before the real attempt. Signs: an unusually small first transfer to a new destination, followed within hours or days by a much larger one to the same place. Legitimate explanations: a cautious customer deliberately testing a new payee before sending the full amount, which is genuinely good practice. The distinguishing question is whether the customer knows they made the small payment.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_repeated_failures_then_success', text: 'Repeated failures then a success. Several transfers rejected for insufficient funds, a frozen account, or a failed check, followed by a successful one. Someone probing for the limit of what will go through. Legitimate explanations: a customer genuinely miscalculating their balance and retrying after a deposit lands. Ask whether the successful amount was sized to sit just under a threshold.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_dormant_reactivation', text: 'Dormant account reactivation. An account with little or no activity for a long period suddenly moves money. Dormant accounts are attractive precisely because nobody is watching them and the owner may not notice for months. Legitimate explanations: a savings account being used for its intended purpose, or a customer returning from abroad. Ask whether the contact details on the account changed shortly before the activity.' },
  { doc_id: 'fraud_guardian_account_misuse', text: 'Misuse of a guardian-supervised account. Unusual outbound activity on an account held by a minor. Only the guardian can move money out, so unexpected outbound activity means either the guardian is misusing funds held for a child or the guardian mailbox is compromised. Legitimate explanations: the guardian genuinely spending on the child behalf. Treat with particular care: the account holder here cannot advocate for themselves.' }
];

return patterns.map(p => ({ json: { doc_id: p.doc_id, text: p.text } }));

// Five documents covering features built in Session 11. The live index was
// silent on all of them, which meant a customer asking "do you pay interest?"
// got routed to a human rather than answered -- the safe failure, but a useless
// one when the answer is published policy.
//
// Additive only: none of these contradict an existing snippet.
const docs = [
  {
    doc_id: 'doc_savings_interest',
    text: 'Savings Interest Policy: Digital Bank pays interest on savings accounts at 5.00% per annum. Checking and business accounts earn 0%. Interest is calculated monthly at one twelfth of the annual rate, applied to the closing balance of the month, and credited to the account automatically -- customers do not need to ask for it or claim it. Interest is only ever paid for a month that has fully ended; a month still in progress is never paid as though it were complete. Amounts are rounded down to the whole paisa, so the bank never pays a fraction of a paisa it did not compute. Each month is paid exactly once: the calculation can safely be re-run without paying twice, and equally without skipping a month if a scheduled run is missed. Interest is paid out of the bank treasury account through the same double-entry mechanism as any other movement of money, so it appears on the customer statement as a normal credit describing the month it covers, and it is included in the nightly reconciliation. If a balance is small enough that one month of interest rounds to less than one paisa, nothing is credited that month.'
  },
  {
    doc_id: 'doc_statements',
    text: 'Account Statement Policy: Customers can request an account statement by email at any time and it is produced and sent automatically, with no human step. The default period is the last full calendar month. A customer can instead ask for this month (month to date), this year (year to date), or an explicit date range such as statement from 2026-08-01 to 2026-08-31. If a customer holds more than one account and does not say which one they mean, Digital Bank asks rather than guessing. A statement shows the opening balance, total money in, total money out, the closing balance, and every individual transaction with the running balance after it, plus any outstanding debt on the account. The figures are rebuilt from the ledger itself rather than from any cached running total, so a statement is reconstructable from the ledger alone. Before a statement is sent, the system verifies that it adds up -- opening balance plus money in, minus money out, must equal the closing balance. If it does not, the statement is not sent and the operations team is alerted instead. Customers who see a transaction they do not recognise on a statement can reply and a dispute will be opened.'
  },
  {
    doc_id: 'doc_dispute_process',
    text: 'Dispute Process Policy: A customer who sees a transaction they did not make should email Digital Bank describing what they are disputing and roughly when it happened, including the transaction reference from their statement if they can find it. The customer immediately receives a dispute reference beginning DSP-. Every dispute is decided by a human specialist; there is no automated dispute resolution, because whether a person actually authorised a payment is not a judgement a machine should make alone. On a joint account the rule about who gets a say is explicit: raising a dispute is unilateral, so any single holder can raise one without the other holders permission, because reporting suspected fraud should never wait on someone else replying. Every other holder is then asked for their side and can agree or disagree with a comment. Neither answer decides the dispute by itself; the specialist decides with all the holder positions in front of them. If a dispute is upheld and a specific transaction was identified, that transaction is reversed. Disputes are typically reviewed within 3 to 5 business days.'
  },
  {
    doc_id: 'doc_reversals_and_debt',
    text: 'Reversal and Debt Policy: Digital Bank has no self-service undo on a completed transfer, and no automated process reverses one. A completed transfer is only ever reversed by a human decision -- either a dispute that a specialist upholds, or a reversal an operator authorises directly. When a reversal is approved, the bank takes back whatever the recipient still holds and returns it to the sender, using the same double-entry mechanism as any transfer. If the recipient has already spent some or all of the money, the bank recovers what is available and records the remainder as a debt owed by the recipient to the bank. A customer balance is never pushed below zero: a shortfall is tracked as a separate debt, not as a negative balance. Outstanding debt is then collected automatically from money the recipient receives afterwards, in instalments if necessary, until it is cleared. Because of this, a reversal may return money to the sender in stages rather than all at once, and the sender is told what was recovered immediately versus what is still being collected. Outstanding debt on an account is shown on statements and is disclosed to anyone being asked to join that account as a holder.'
  },
  {
    doc_id: 'doc_holder_changes',
    text: 'Adding and Removing Account Holders Policy: A holder can be added to an existing joint account, and a holder can be removed from one. Both require recorded consent, collected by emailing each person a reference code beginning JNT- that they answer by replying APPROVE or REJECT. Adding a holder requires every existing holder to approve, and it also requires the person being added to accept -- joint liability is not something other people can sign someone up for. The invitation to join discloses everything the incoming holder would be taking on, including any active hold on the account, any outstanding loan balance, and any unpaid debt. Removing a holder requires every holder to agree, including the person being removed: nobody can be taken off an account they are liable for without agreeing, and nobody can walk away from one unilaterally. A removal is refused outright in three situations, and the reason is always stated: when the account has an outstanding loan or an active hold, because the set of people answerable for a debt cannot be reduced while the debt exists; when removal would leave the account with no holders at all, in which case the account should be closed instead; and when it would remove the guardian from an account a minor still holds. Removal does not divide or reallocate any share of a balance, hold or standing order. Separately, the transfer mandate (either-or versus all-signatures) and the closure rule (unanimous versus majority) can both be changed after an account is opened, by unanimous agreement of all holders. Majority closure is only available on accounts with three or more holders, because with two holders a majority is both of them, which is what unanimous already means.'
  }
];

return docs.map(d => ({ json: { doc_id: d.doc_id, text: d.text } }));
